\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We presented a sample-select-correct pipeline for frozen flow-matching VLAs with a lightweight CRM trained on task-specific VLA rollout data.
On in-distribution LIBERO tasks, correction is the active mechanism ($+$5.3\,pp at $K{=}10$, 68\% of the gain at $K{=}5$), with multi-sampling as an enabling condition that provides centering for bounded correction.
Three distribution shifts (input, task, feature) form a unified failure framework: the CRM degrades when its input departs from the training distribution. Task shift is proximity-dependent: near-OOD produces adversarial degradation ($-$12\,pp), while far-OOD is neutral.
A $K{=}2$ $L_2$ probe ($\rho{=}0.9979$ vs.\ $K{=}10$ variance) enables adaptive compute allocation, applying full correction only when the VLA is uncertain.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
All experiments use LIBERO in simulation with SmolVLA (450M); real-robot transfer and larger VLAs are untested. Absolute success rates (17.6\% Object, 34.3\% Spatial) are far from deployment-ready; A2C2~\cite{a2c2_2025} reports 67--72\% with full SmolVLA training. The CRM requires task-specific calibration data and does not generalize to unseen tasks. Long-horizon cross-suite improvement ($+$4.2\,pp) is driven by a single task sharing primitives with the training set.

